## 4. Plans and records

PLAN describes milestones and dependencies; TODO is the short checklist of current work. The coordinator uses the **plan** and **todo** skills when their creation is needed within authorized work, and maintains them as progress or blockers change. Write important decisions into their owning document when settled; conversation alone is not a durable record.

The coordinator uses the **audit** skill for one brief timestamped record at a milestone boundary or when coordinated work that changed files completes, stops blocked, or is abandoned. Record actual participants, significant attempts, results, and remaining work. Preserve earlier audits. Workers supply evidence rather than separate logs. No hourly updates or progress screenshots are required.

## 5. Verification

Keep the existing working user journey intact. Use the **tests** skill when writing, changing, or reviewing tests. Verify the changed behavior and its relevant failure cases; do not multiply tests or repeat passing checks without a concrete reason. Report what was implemented, what was actually checked, and what remains unverified. Never invent user approval.

Testing levels, every-commit commands, the release-test brief, and the record of what was verified at which commit are in [PLACEHOLDER: TESTING path, if it exists]. Before work is committed, the README is revisited so it matches the code. Release candidates are tagged by the user only; agents recommend them.

